<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ross-on-Wye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ross-on-Wye is a market town in Herefordshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on a sandstone cliff above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a>. It developed on an estate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> and is known for its 17th-century market hall. The Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariconium">Ariconium</a> lay a few kilometres to the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ross-on-wye.jpg" alt="Ross-on-Wye" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Halling · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_River_Wye_and_Ross-on-Wye_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7415567.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
